{"id": "q5", "claim": "c2", "kind": "claim", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s5"], "question": "In the paper's instruction-carrier analysis, suppose OCR instruction mismatches were eliminated entirely while the number of successful OCR jailbreaks stayed the same. What would OCR's CASR become, and would the TEXT–OCR CASR gap close?", "answer": "OCR CASR would equal OCR ASR, 40.59%, because with zero mismatches the CASR denominator equals N. TEXT CASR is 50.89%, so the gap would grow to about 10.3 points instead of closing. Removing perception failures therefore does not explain away OCR's lower vulnerability.", "rubric": ["States that OCR CASR would become about 40.59% (equal to its ASR) and the gap would not close; it would widen to roughly 10 points.", "Reasons that with no mismatches the CASR denominator equals the full response count, so CASR equals ASR.", "Compares against TEXT CASR of about 50.89%."]}
,
{"id": "q6", "claim": "c2", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s9", "s4"], "question": "A critic argues that Story framing's very high ASR for some models in the paper (for example glm4.1v-9b) is inflated, because responses that tell a fictional story containing only high-level, non-operational hints would be counted as jailbreak successes. Using the paper's harmfulness rubric, its success threshold, and its judge rules, why is this critique wrong?", "answer": "Under the paper's 1–5 rubric, high-level or partial harmful information scores 3. ASR counts only scores of 4 or higher, which requires actionable or detailed operational help, so such stories would not count as successes. The anti-deception rules stop fictional packaging from lowering the score of genuinely harmful content, but they do not raise the score of non-actionable content.", "rubric": ["Concludes that such responses would not be counted as successes, so the critique fails.", "States that high-level or partial information scores 3, while success requires a score of at least 4 (actionable assistance).", "Notes that the anti-deception rules only prevent fictional or academic framing from reducing scores when harmful information is present, and do not inflate scores."]}
,
{"id": "q7", "claim": "c3", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s5"], "question": "Someone reads the paper as showing that proprietary-access MLLMs are safer than open-weight ones. Using the paper's per-model average ASRs, how many of the eight proprietary-access models have a higher average ASR than the most robust open-weight model? What does this imply for the reading?", "answer": "The most robust open-weight model is qwen3-vl-8b at 19.84%. Six of the eight proprietary models exceed it: glm-4.6v, qwen3-vl-plus, doubao-seed-2.0-lite, kimi-k2.5, gemini-3-flash and grok-4-fast. Only claude-sonnet-4.5 and gpt-5 are lower. The reading is therefore unsupported, and robustness is model-dependent rather than determined by access type.", "rubric": ["Identifies qwen3-vl-8b (about 19.84%) as the most robust open-weight model.", "States that 6 of the 8 proprietary models have higher average ASR (only claude-sonnet-4.5 and gpt-5 are lower).", "Concludes that access type does not determine safety, which is consistent with model-dependent alignment."]}
,
{"id": "q15", "claim": "c5", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s5"], "question": "In the paper's visual semantic ablation, take the largest ASR increase among the control image conditions as an estimate of the effect of mere image presence. Roughly what fraction of the authorization-document condition's ASR increase over the no-image baseline is left to attribute to semantic content? What does this support?", "answer": "The largest control effect is the blank image, at +2.19 points, and authorization documents add +12.96 points. About 2.19/12.96 ≈ 17% could reflect image presence, leaving roughly 83% (about 10.8 points) attributable to semantic meaning. This supports the paper's conclusion that the vulnerability stems from contextual meaning such as authority cues, not from visual input itself.", "rubric": ["Gives about 83% (about 10.8 of 12.96 points) attributable to semantics, or equivalently about 17% due to presence.", "Uses the blank-image control (+2.19) and the authorization-document increase (+12.96).", "Concludes that the increase comes mainly from semantic or authority content rather than image presence."]}
,
{"id": "q17", "claim": "c6", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s10"], "question": "A reader suggests that OCR's lower ASR in the paper comes from a few harm domains whose rendered instructions are especially hard to handle. In the paper's category-level carrier results, which subcategory shows the smallest TEXT-minus-OCR gap? Does even that subcategory support the reader's suggestion?", "answer": "The smallest gap is in Unlicensed Medical & Legal Advice, at about 5.81 points. Even this minimum is positive, and all subcategories range up to about 12.46 points. The carrier effect is therefore broad-based across harm intents rather than concentrated in a few domains, so the suggestion is not supported.", "rubric": ["Identifies Unlicensed Medical & Legal Advice (about 5.8 points) as the smallest gap.", "Notes that even the smallest gap is positive, and all categories favor TEXT (roughly 6–12 points).", "Rejects the suggestion that the effect is concentrated in a few domains."]}
,
{"id": "q20", "claim": "c7", "kind": "claim", "form": "change-condition", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s6"], "question": "The paper ties its attention diagnostics in gemma3-12b to a specific layer singled out by its representation diagnostics. Which layer is that, and why was it chosen? If, at that layer, authority-document contexts had shown higher attention to harm-related task-label tokens than danger-image contexts, how would the paper's internal explanation be affected?", "answer": "The layer is 42, where authority-versus-danger representation divergence shows its sharpest rise. The paper's explanation rests on the consistency between representation displacement and reduced attention mass on harm-label (and visual) tokens at that layer during response initiation. Higher harm-label attention there would break that consistency and undercut the internal account of authority-cue susceptibility.", "rubric": ["Identifies layer 42 as the layer where representation divergence rises most sharply.", "Concludes that the explanation would be weakened or contradicted.", "Explains that the account depends on lower attention to harm-related tokens coinciding with the representation shift at that layer."]}
,
{"id": "q21", "claim": "c7", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s6"], "question": "In the paper's representation-level diagnostics on gemma3-12b, which two visual conditions define the fitted direction, and how is that direction tested beyond the data used to find it? What additional claim does the per-domain result support that the generalization test alone does not?", "answer": "The direction is authority-document minus danger-image, fit on discovery samples. It is then applied, fixed, to held-out pairs, where authority contexts are clearly displaced along it. The held-out test shows the pattern is not a sample artifact. The positive shifts in every harm domain further show it is not driven by particular domains, supporting a shared higher-layer authority representation across diverse harmful objectives.", "rubric": ["Identifies the direction as authority-document versus danger-image contexts.", "Describes fitting on discovery samples and applying the fixed direction to held-out pairs, with displacement observed.", "Explains that positive shifts across all harm domains support a shared pattern across harmful objectives, beyond sample-level generalization."]}
]}

