{"id": "q15", "claim": "c5", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s5"], "question": "In the paper's visual semantic ablation, treat the largest ASR increase among the control image conditions as an estimate of the effect of mere image presence. Which control condition is that? How many ASR points of the dangerous-context condition's increase over the no-image baseline would then remain attributable to semantic content? What does this imply for the paper's claim about where the visual vulnerability comes from?", "answer": "The largest control effect is the blank image, at +2.19 points. Dangerous context, the weakest semantic condition, adds +8.45 points, leaving about 6.26 points (roughly 74%) attributable to semantics. Since even the weakest semantic condition clearly exceeds every control, the increase is driven mainly by contextual meaning rather than by the presence of an image.", "rubric": ["Identifies the blank image (about +2.19 points) as the largest control effect.", "Computes about 6.3 points (roughly 74% of the +8.45 dangerous-context increase) as attributable to semantics.", "Concludes that this supports the vulnerability coming mainly from semantic visual meaning rather than from image presence."]},
